.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.git
# `**/` on every one of these, because a .dockerignore pattern with no slash and
# no `**` matches only at the context ROOT. Plain `__pycache__` therefore excluded
# a hypothetical /__pycache__ and nothing else, while the demo stage COPYs
# examples/memory-ui/{server,demo}/ wholesale — so the host's cpython-311 .pyc
# files went into an image whose interpreter is 3.11. COPY preserves mtimes, and a
# .pyc whose embedded mtime and size match its .py is used without recompiling, so
# the container could execute bytecode that does not match the source sitting next
# to it in the image. Verified the anchoring directly: with the old patterns a
# nested marker.pyc arrived in the image; with `**/` none does.
**/.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.tox
# node_modules is 75 MB and frontend/ ~89 MB of a 92 MB context, none of which any
# stage copies — the root build only takes examples/memory-ui/{server,demo}/. The
# frontend image builds from its own context (docker-compose.yml) and has its own
# .dockerignore. Excluding it here keeps `npm install` from invalidating the root
# build context and re-transferring ~90 MB on the "first build takes a few
# minutes" path.
**/node_modules
examples/memory-ui/frontend/
tests/
docs/
*.md
!README.md
dist/
.claude/
misc/
